-- ============================================================================
-- Lumina LMS — Consolidated PostgreSQL Schema (final intended state)
-- ============================================================================
-- Generated by merging:
--   server/database/schema-postgres.sql            (base schema, Postgres)
--   server/database/create-accounting.sql            (seed data only, no DDL)
--   server/database/migrate-add-accounting.sql       (MySQL DDL -> translated)
--   server/database/migrate-add-all-features.sql     (MySQL DDL -> translated)
--   server/database/migrate-add-application-status.sql (MySQL DDL -> translated)
--   server/database/migrate-add-assignment-grades.sql (Postgres, already valid)
--   server/database/migrate-add-attendance.sql       (MySQL DDL -> translated)
--   server/database/migrate-add-departments.sql / migrate-add-departments-postgres.js
--   server/database/migrate-add-fee-due-date.js      (Postgres, inline SQL)
--   server/database/migrate-add-feature-flags.sql    (MySQL DDL -> translated)
--   server/database/migrate-add-mailbox.sql          (MySQL DDL -> translated)
--   server/database/migrate-add-password-reset.sql   (MySQL DDL -> translated)
--   server/database/migrate-add-total-years.sql      (Postgres, inline SQL)
--   server/database/modules-queries.sql              (MySQL DDL -> translated; superseded by schema-postgres.sql)
--   server/database/complete-mysql-to-postgres-import.js (already-translated
--       Postgres DDL for every feature table + ENUM types; used as the primary
--       source of truth for the Postgres column types/enum names below because
--       it is the most complete, most recently written, already-valid script)
--
-- This file is idempotent (CREATE ... IF NOT EXISTS / DO $$ EXCEPTION guards)
-- and can be run top-to-bottom against an empty database.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- NOTES / ISSUES FOUND WHILE CONSOLIDATING (read before using)
-- ============================================================================
-- 1. MIXED DIALECTS IN SOURCE: This repo is mid-migration from MariaDB to
--    Postgres. Several migrate-*.sql files use MySQL-only syntax even though
--    they are meant to run against Postgres:
--      - Inline `ENUM(...)` column types            -> translated to CREATE TYPE ... AS ENUM
--      - `ALTER TABLE ... MODIFY role ENUM(...)`     -> no-op here; the base
--        schema-postgres.sql already defines user_role with 'accounting'
--        included, so migrate-add-accounting.sql's MODIFY statement was
--        redundant/never valid against Postgres in the first place.
--      - `AFTER <column>` clauses on ADD COLUMN      -> dropped (Postgres has
--        no concept of column ordering via ALTER TABLE)
--      - `ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP`                -> replaced with the
--        update_updated_at_column() trigger pattern already established in
--        schema-postgres.sql, applied to every table that has an updated_at
--        column added by a later migration
--      - `ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb4 ...`  -> dropped (MySQL-only)
--      - `INDEX idx_x (col)` inline in CREATE TABLE   -> converted to a
--        separate `CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS ... ON table(col)`
--      - `UNIQUE KEY name (cols)` inline              -> converted to
--        `UNIQUE (cols)` table constraint
--      - `JSON` columns                                -> `JSONB` (matches
--        complete-mysql-to-postgres-import.js, which already made this call)
--
-- 2. BUG IN complete-mysql-to-postgres-import.js: that script (and by
--    extension every migrate-*.sql it was derived from) creates indexes with
--    generic names re-used across many tables, e.g. `idx_student`,
--    `idx_course`, `idx_status`, `idx_created`, `idx_user`. In MySQL, index
--    names only need to be unique per-table, so this was fine. In PostgreSQL,
--    index names must be unique per-schema. Because every CREATE INDEX in
--    that script uses `IF NOT EXISTS`, the *first* table to claim e.g.
--    `idx_student` succeeds, and every subsequent `CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS
--    idx_student ON <other_table>(student_id)` SILENTLY NO-OPS — the index is
--    never created on that table. This means, as originally written, most
--    tables after the first collision (accounting_tasks.idx_student,
--    attendance.idx_student, payments.idx_student, class_schedules.idx_student,
--    video_progress.idx_student, student_achievements.idx_student,
--    quiz_attempts.idx_student, assignment_submissions.idx_student,
--    course_enrollments.idx_student, and the equivalent collisions for
--    idx_course, idx_status, idx_created, idx_user, idx_assigned_to, etc.)
--    are silently missing their indexes in a real deployment. This
--    consolidated file FIXES that by giving every index a table-qualified
--    name (e.g. idx_attendance_student, idx_payments_student). Call this out
--    to the .NET/EF Core team: any EF Core migration that mirrors "the
--    existing index set" from a live DB inspection may be missing indexes
--    that were *intended* but never actually created — worth deciding
--    whether to add them fresh rather than faithfully reproduce the bug.
--
-- 3. MISSING FOREIGN KEYS IN SOURCE: several feature tables added by
--    migrate-add-all-features.sql declare FK-shaped columns (course_id,
--    student_id, author_id, created_by, quiz_id, question_id, attempt_id,
--    thread_id, category_id, module_id, video_id, achievement_id, user_id)
--    but never add an actual `FOREIGN KEY ... REFERENCES` constraint:
--    quizzes, quiz_questions, quiz_question_options, quiz_attempts,
--    quiz_answers, forum_categories, forum_threads, forum_replies,
--    certificates, notifications, activity_logs, analytics_events,
--    course_videos, student_achievements (FK present here, unlike siblings),
--    video_progress (FK NOT present despite video_id/student_id columns).
--    This is preserved as-is below (commented `-- no FK in source`) rather
--    than inventing constraints that were never enforced in production,
--    since student_id/user_id here is sometimes a polymorphic reference
--    (see user_type/user_type_enum: 'user' vs 'student' — the same numeric
--    column can point at either `users` or `students`, which a single FK
--    cannot express anyway). Decide per-table in the EF Core model whether
--    to add real FKs now that you have a clean slate.
--
-- 4. NO SEPARATE faculty/accounting_users TABLE: 'faculty' and 'accounting'
--    are just values of the `role` column on `users` (see create-faculty.js,
--    create-accounting.sql, migrate-add-accounting.sql). There is exactly one
--    users table for all staff-side roles (admin/faculty/accounting), and a
--    separate `students` table for the student/parent-facing side. Several
--    "user_id" columns (activity_logs, notifications, analytics_events,
--    password_reset_tokens) are polymorphic and paired with a `user_type`
--    enum ('user' | 'student') to say which table they point into.
--
-- 5. PASSWORDS ARE PLAIN TEXT in `users.password` / `students.password` — see
--    service-catalog.md for details; this is a schema-level concern too
--    since the column is only VARCHAR(255) with no indication of a hash
--    format (no bcrypt cost prefix, etc.) — seed data literally stores
--    'admin', 'faculty', 'accounting', '1234' as passwords.
--
-- 6. modules-queries.sql duplicates course_modules/module_materials already
--    defined in schema-postgres.sql with identical shape (it's a reference
--    query-cookbook file, not an independent migration) — no new columns.
-- ============================================================================


-- ============================================================================
